<main>

    <section class="hero">
        <h1 class="fade-up fade-up-2">Install</h1>
        <p class="tagline fade-up fade-up-3">One command gets you a hub and a vault. Connect your AI, paste a starter prompt, and it builds the rest with you.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Get started &mdash; one command</h2>

        <p>Requires <a href="https://bun.sh" target="_blank">Bun</a> 1.3 or later (<code>bun --version</code> to check, <code>bun upgrade</code> to update). Then run:</p>

        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>bun add -g @openparachute/hub &amp;&amp; parachute init</code>
        </div>

        <p><code>parachute init</code> starts the hub on port <code>1939</code>, offers to expose it beyond your laptop (Cloudflare Tunnel is the default on a server &mdash; it'll offer to install <code>cloudflared</code> for you on Linux), installs the vault module quietly, and opens a browser setup wizard at <code>/admin/setup</code>: <strong>Account &rarr; Vault &rarr; Expose &rarr; Done</strong>. Create an admin login, then create your first vault (or import one from a git repo, or skip &mdash; vault creation lives in the wizard, not the CLI). If an expose attempt fails, <code>init</code> doesn't abort &mdash; it keeps going on the loopback URL and prints a retry hint (<code>parachute expose public --cloudflare</code>). When the hub <em>is</em> exposed publicly and no admin exists yet, <code>init</code> prints a one-time <strong>bootstrap token</strong> &mdash; the wizard asks for it before it lets you create the first admin (loopback installs skip this). It's idempotent &mdash; safe to re-run. Check the stack any time with <code>parachute status</code>.</p>

        <aside class="upgrade-callout">
            <strong>Installed during the beta via the old <code>@openparachute/cli</code>?</strong>
            <p>Run the rename once before <code>parachute init</code>:</p>
            <div class="code-inline">
                <code>bun remove -g @openparachute/cli &amp;&amp; bun add -g @openparachute/hub &amp;&amp; parachute upgrade vault</code>
            </div>
            <p>The package was renamed in April 2026; vault migrations run automatically on the next boot. Coming from the launch Vault 0.2.4? The <a href="/preview/vault-0.4.5-arc/">0.2.4 &rarr; 0.4.5 upgrade arc</a> has the full picture. From here on, <code>parachute init</code> is the front door.</p>
        </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="connect-mcp-clients">
        <h2>Connect your AI</h2>

        <p>The wizard's done screen hands you a copy-pasteable <code>claude mcp add</code> command with a freshly-minted token &mdash; <strong>Claude Code is auto-configured</strong> by the install on the hub machine. Start a new Claude Code session and run <code>/mcp</code> to confirm the vault appears.</p>

        <p>Connecting any other client &mdash; Claude.ai on the web, Codex, Goose, Cursor, Zed, your own agent &mdash; comes down to one URL. Point the client at your vault's MCP endpoint (ChatGPT and similar web UIs call this a <strong>connector</strong>):</p>

        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;/mcp</code>
        </div>

        <p><code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;</code> is the address your hub answers on (the same URL you sign in at); <code>&lt;name&gt;</code> is your vault name (<code>default</code> unless you changed it). The <strong><code>/mcp</code> suffix is required</strong> &mdash; the bare path is a metadata page, not the endpoint. OAuth-capable clients send you through hub sign-in automatically; for clients that need a bearer token, mint one from the vault admin SPA's Tokens page. Full per-client steps and the bearer-token path are in <a href="/docs/">the docs</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Bootstrap your vault</h2>

        <div class="starter-callout">
            <h3>&#9733; Paste one prompt &mdash; your AI builds your vault</h3>
            <p>Your vault starts empty. The fastest way to fill it: open <strong>the set-up-your-vault starter prompt</strong>, paste it into your MCP-connected AI, and it inspects what's there, interviews you about how you think, and proposes a tag and path structure &mdash; then builds it on your say-so. This is the payoff. Start here.</p>
            <a href="/onboarding/vault-setup/" class="button">Open the starter prompt &rarr;</a>
            <p class="starter-adjacent">Next steps from there: a second prompt that <a href="/onboarding/surface-build/">builds a custom UI</a> over your vault, or <a href="/scripting/">scripting your vault</a> from bash, Python, or JS for imports, cron jobs, and CI.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>See your notes <span style="font-weight:400;color:var(--fg-dim);">(optional)</span></h2>

        <p>Want to browse, search, and edit your notes in a UI &mdash; and watch the graph form? Install <strong>Surface</strong>, the UI host module. It auto-bootstraps Notes as its first surface:</p>

        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>parachute install surface</code>
        </div>

        <p>Open <a href="http://localhost:1939/surface/notes/" target="_blank"><code>localhost:1939/surface/notes/</code></a>, click the vault popover, and hit <strong>Connect</strong> next to your vault. You get note browsing, search, tag views, a graph visualization, voice capture, and a markdown editor &mdash; multi-vault, one install for every vault on your hub. The source is open (Vite + React + TypeScript); fork it or build your own UI under the same host.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Going further <span style="font-weight:400;color:var(--fg-dim);">(optional)</span></h2>

        <p><strong>Run it always-on.</strong> Your laptop sleeps; a small server doesn't. Three peer paths to a hub that stays up &mdash; same architecture, same wizard, ~five minutes each:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><a href="/deploy/render/">Deploy to Render &rarr;</a> &mdash; one-click, your own subdomain, ~$7/month.</li>
            <li><a href="/deploy/fly/">Deploy to Fly &rarr;</a> &mdash; CLI-first peer path, ~$3.34/month, portable volume snapshots.</li>
            <li><strong>Your own Linux box</strong> (EC2, Hetzner, any VPS) &mdash; same <code>bun add -g @openparachute/hub &amp;&amp; parachute init</code>, then finish in the wizard. On an SSH'd server <code>init</code> pre-selects public exposure so the wizard URL is reachable.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Your own box works the same way Render does.</strong> Parachute runs one way everywhere: <code>parachute serve</code> &mdash; the hub in the foreground with every module supervised as a child process &mdash; kept alive by the platform's own process manager. On Render or Fly that's the container runtime; on your own machine, <code>parachute init</code> installs the hub as a managed service (a <strong>systemd</strong> unit on a Linux box, a <strong>launchd</strong> agent on a Mac) that keeps it running and supervises the modules. So a self-hosted EC2 or Hetzner box gets the same always-on, survives-a-reboot, manage-it-from-the-admin-UI behavior the hosted deploys do &mdash; you own the box instead of renting the container. No PID-hunting, no manual restarts: the manager brings the hub back after a crash or a reboot, and the hub brings every module back with it.</p>

        <p>Already running an older install that spawned the hub as a detached background process? <code>parachute migrate --to-supervised</code> moves it onto the managed-service model in place &mdash; one command, idempotent, no data migration.</p>

        <p><strong>Reach it from other devices.</strong> A <a href="https://tailscale.com" target="_blank">Tailnet</a> (<code>tailscale serve</code>) is the smooth private path &mdash; your hub, visible only to your own Tailscale devices. Public HTTPS (Cloudflare Tunnel or Tailscale Funnel) works too and is what claude.ai needs, but we're still polishing those edges &mdash; the <a href="/deploy/render/">Render</a> and <a href="/deploy/fly/">Fly</a> deploys are the easiest way to a clean public URL today.</p>

        <p><strong>Back it up &amp; script it.</strong> Export your whole vault to portable, git-trackable markdown with <code>parachute-vault export &lt;dir&gt;</code> (and <code>import</code> back) &mdash; disaster recovery, vault migration, or editing in another tool. Driving the vault from a script, cron job, or importer is covered in <a href="/scripting/">Scripting your vault</a>. Hosting for friends and family? See the <a href="/multi-user/">multi-user operator guide</a>.</p>

        <p><strong>Stay current.</strong> <code>parachute upgrade</code> sweeps the hub and every installed service to the latest release; <code>parachute upgrade vault</code> (or <code>surface</code>, <code>scribe</code>) does one. Add <code>--channel rc</code> to track release candidates. Vault migrations run automatically on first post-upgrade boot.</p>

        <p><strong>Want Parachute Cloud?</strong> A hosted version &mdash; same architecture, no server to run &mdash; is in the works. <a href="https://openparachute.substack.com" target="_blank">Subscribe for updates</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>

        <details>
            <summary>Something not working?</summary>
            <ul style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                <li><strong>Claude Code doesn't see the vault.</strong> Start a new session (MCP config loads at session start) and run <code>/mcp</code>. Still missing? Re-run <code>parachute-vault mcp-install</code> on the hub machine.</li>
                <li><strong>Port 1940 already in use.</strong> Run <code>lsof -i :1940</code> to see what's there; change ports in <code>~/.parachute/vault/.env</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>A client can't reach the MCP endpoint.</strong> Confirm the URL ends in <code>/mcp</code> and points at the <em>hub</em> origin (the address you sign in at), not the vault's own port <code>1940</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>Stuck on Bun 1.2.x?</strong> You may see a cosmetic <code>InvalidPackageResolution</code> or <code>FileNotFound</code> warning &mdash; the package installs anyway. Upgrading to Bun 1.3 clears it.</li>
                <li>
                    <strong>The Claude connector fails right after consent, over a Cloudflare tunnel.</strong>
                    You exposed your hub publicly with a <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/products/tunnel/" target="_blank">Cloudflare Tunnel</a> (<code>parachute expose public --cloudflare</code>) and tried to add your vault to the <strong>Claude Desktop / claude.ai / mobile MCP connector</strong>. OAuth consent succeeds, then Claude reports <em>&ldquo;the integration rejected the credentials&rdquo;</em> or <em>&ldquo;the connection was reverted&rdquo;</em> and never reaches the vault &mdash; while Notes and Claude Code (which use a static token) keep working fine, so it looks like an OAuth bug.
                    <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;"><strong>Cause &mdash; this is a Cloudflare edge setting, not a Parachute bug.</strong> Consent runs in your <em>browser</em>, so it passes Cloudflare. But the token exchange (<code>POST /oauth/token</code>) and the MCP calls are <em>server-to-server</em> requests from Anthropic's servers &mdash; and Cloudflare's <strong>Bot Fight Mode</strong> / <strong>Super Bot Fight Mode</strong> / <strong>Browser Integrity Check</strong> (or a Cloudflare Access policy) challenges them with a <code>403</code> &ldquo;Access denied&rdquo; page. Claude gets the 403 instead of a token.</p>
                    <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;"><strong>Fix &mdash; in your Cloudflare dashboard for the exposed zone:</strong></p>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
                        <li>Turn <strong>off</strong> Bot Fight Mode / Super Bot Fight Mode (Security &rarr; Bots), <em>or</em> add a <strong>WAF Skip rule</strong> scoped to your exposed hostname (or the API paths <code>/oauth/*</code>, <code>/.well-known/*</code>, <code>/vault/*</code>) that skips Bot Fight Mode, Managed Rules, and the Browser Integrity Check.</li>
                        <li>If <strong>Browser Integrity Check</strong> is on, disable it (or skip it for those paths).</li>
                        <li>If you put the hostname behind <strong>Cloudflare Access (Zero Trust)</strong>, remove that policy &mdash; the hub does its own OAuth, and an outer Access gate blocks the API.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;"><strong>Confirm it's this.</strong> From any non-browser tool, hit your public origin with a non-browser user-agent &mdash; e.g. <code>curl -A python-requests https://&lt;your-host&gt;/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server</code>. If Cloudflare's <code>403</code> &ldquo;Access denied&rdquo; HTML page comes back (rather than a hub response) while a normal browser loads the same URL fine, that's the cause. The failed token exchange also won't appear in <code>~/.parachute/hub/logs/hub.log</code> &mdash; Cloudflare blocked it before it ever reached the hub.</p>
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">Found something we missed? <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-vault/issues" target="_blank">Open an issue</a>.</p>
        </details>
    </section>

